Files
description: a whole text file of a given language - the extension names the concrete
- >
  The node *is* a file, and the FILENAME EXTENSION names the concrete: `file/json` (`.json`),
  `file/json5` (`.json5`), `file/json5p` (`.json5p`), `file/yaml` (`.yaml` / `.yml`),
  `file/yamlover` (`.yo`, plus the legacy `.yamlover` spelling - read forever, never written).
  Interior nodes take the same language without the `file/` prefix - the inlined forms. A file
  and a subdirectory are equivalent representations of one node.
- >
  A non-data **text material** - markdown, asciidoc, csv, plantuml - is modeled as a
  `file/yaml` scalar: its single value is the raw text, kept verbatim and rendered by its
  `format`. The format carries the real material type (`text/markdown`); the concrete only
  records "a text file holding one scalar", so the same modeling covers every material the
  system will ever meet.
- >
  An extension the tables do not know - or none at all - is SNIFFED rather than assumed: a NUL
  byte in the head, or a size past a megabyte, makes it the binary concrete below; anything else
  is read as text and modeled as a `file/yaml` scalar, its content parsed so a lone `42` is a
  number and a lone `Alice` a string. The metadata always outranks the guess: a `meta.yo` member
  clause DECLARING the decode concrete (`concrete: yamlover/stream` parses a `.txt` as yamlover;
  `concrete: text/utf-8` decodes it to a string; [the members clause](*::meta:members)) wins over
  extension and sniff alike, and `type: binary` alone still forces bytes - "read whatever you
  have so this type comes out".
